{"format":"hologram.registry.artifact/v1","id":"mcr.microsoft.com/cbl-mariner/base/ruby","fetched":"2026-09-25T12:12:11.321Z","source":{"name":"Microsoft Artifact Registry","api":"https://mcr.microsoft.com/api/v1/catalog/cbl-mariner/base/ruby/details?reg=mar","page":"https://mcr.microsoft.com/en-us/product/cbl-mariner/base/ruby/about"},"readme":"***Warning: Azure Linux 2.0 will reach its End of Life (EOL) on July 31st, 2025. After this date, it will no longer receive updates, security patches, or support, which could leave your systems vulnerable. We strongly recommend upgrading to an Azure Linux 3.0 image before July 31st, 2025.***\n\n## About\n\nThis repository contains the official Ruby image for CBL-Mariner.\n\n### What is CBL-Mariner?\n\nCBL-Mariner is an internal Linux distribution for Microsoft’s cloud infrastructure and edge products and services. CBL-Mariner is designed to provide a consistent platform for these devices and services and will enhance Microsoft’s ability to stay current on Linux updates.  \n\nTo learn more about CBL-Mariner, refer to our [GitHub repository](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/).\n\n### What is Ruby?\n\nRuby is a dynamic, reflective, object-oriented, general-purpose, open-source programming language. According to its authors, Ruby was influenced by Perl, Smalltalk, Eiffel, Ada, and Lisp. It supports multiple programming paradigms, including functional, object-oriented, and imperative. It also has a dynamic type system and automatic memory management.\n\nSee: [Ruby (programming language)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ruby_%28programming_language%29)\n\n## Featured tags\n\n- `3`\n- `3-cm2.0`\n\nThe featured tags are multi-architecture tags (i.e., `x86-64` and `aarch64`) that refer to the latest version of the image. Additional tags that are specific to ruby version, Mariner build, or architecture can be found in the Tags section.\n\n## Usage\n\n### Running a simple Ruby app\n\n`1.` Create a Dockerfile in the root of your app (next to the Gemfile) to generate a new image that includes the necessary content.\n\n    FROM mcr.microsoft.com/cbl-mariner/base/ruby:3\n\n    # throw errors if Gemfile has been modified since Gemfile.lock\n    RUN bundle config --global frozen 1\n\n    WORKDIR /usr/src/app\n\n    COPY Gemfile Gemfile.lock ./\n    RUN bundle install\n\n    COPY . .\n\n    CMD [\"./your-daemon-or-script.rb\"]\n\n`2.` Build and run your container image.\n\n    docker build -t ruby-app-image .\n    docker run -it --name running-ruby-script ruby-app-image\n\n#### Generate a Gemfile.lock\n\nThe above example Dockerfile expects a Gemfile.lock in your app directory. This docker run will help you generate one. Run it in the root of your app, next to the Gemfile:\n\n    docker run --rm -v \"$PWD\":/usr/src/app -w /usr/src/app mcr.microsoft.com/cbl-mariner/base/ruby:3 bundle install\n\nThe default container startup command is `irb`.\n\n### Run a single Ruby script\n\nFor many simple, single file projects, you may find it inconvenient to write a complete Dockerfile. In such cases, you can run a Ruby script (named my-script.rb in the example below) by using the Mariner based Ruby docker image directly:\n\n    docker run -it --rm --name running-ruby-script -v \"$PWD\":/usr/src/myapp -w /usr/src/myapp mcr.microsoft.com/cbl-mariner/base/ruby:3 ruby my-script.rb\n\n### Run Ruby repl environment\n\nYou may want to simply run the interactive shell that comes with Ruby by launching it when the container starts. In such case, you can run irl (Interactive Ruby Shell) by using the Mariner based Ruby docker image directly. The following command will launch the virtual environment that Ruby comes with:\n\n    docker run -it mcr.microsoft.com/cbl-mariner/base/ruby:3\n\n### Launch Mariner shell\n\nThe Ruby image ships with a bash shell. The launch command can be provided to docker run command to launch Mariner shell. The following command will launch the shell:\n\n    docker run -it mcr.microsoft.com/cbl-mariner/base/ruby:3 /bin/bash\n\n## Support\n\nCBL-Mariner container images are supported only for internal Microsoft use. For more information please see our [Support Statement](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/blob/2.0/SUPPORT.md).\n\n## License\n\nThe CBL-Mariner distribution is provided under the [MIT license](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/blob/2.0/LICENSE). Individual packages and components are governed by their own licenses included in the package sources available via srpm on [packages.microsoft.com](https://packages.microsoft.com/cbl-mariner/) or via our [GitHub repository](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/).\n\n- Legal Notice: [Container License Information](https://aka.ms/mcr/osslegalnotice)","readmeBase":null,"publisher":{"name":"Microsoft","url":"https://mcr.microsoft.com/","official":true,"verified":true},"registered":null,"updated":"2026-07-21T16:55:38.6357913+00:00","categories":[],"keywords":[],"license":"MIT","links":[],"maintainers":[],"stats":{"pulls":null,"pullsText":"305K+","stars":null},"mediaTypes":[],"contentTypes":[],"storage":null,"tags":[{"name":"3.1-cm2.0","digest":"sha256:33f67d8d408cfbbf5bbd4146d8f279ea71b46ceb22822a9fa032eca7a8108b9a","pushed":"2026-03-12T22:19:11.0000000+00:00","size":null,"platforms":[],"mediaType":"application/vnd.docker.distribution.manifest.list.v2+json","status":null},{"name":"3.1","digest":"sha256:33f67d8d408cfbbf5bbd4146d8f279ea71b46ceb22822a9fa032eca7a8108b9a","pushed":"2026-03-12T22:18:59.0000000+00:00","size":null,"platforms":[],"mediaType":"application/vnd.docker.distribution.manifest.list.v2+json","status":null},{"name":"3-cm2.0","digest":"sha256:33f67d8d408cfbbf5bbd4146d8f279ea71b46ceb22822a9fa032eca7a8108b9a","pushed":"2026-03-12T22:18:51.0000000+00:00","size":null,"platforms":[],"mediaType":"application/vnd.docker.distribution.manifest.list.v2+json","status":null},{"name":"3","digest":"sha256:33f67d8d408cfbbf5bbd4146d8f279ea71b46ceb22822a9fa032eca7a8108b9a","pushed":"2026-03-12T